# November 24, 2026: sixteen places spread across New Brunswick: Saint Andrews, Fredericton and the river (Belmont, Gagetown,
# Woodstock), Saint John's King Square, Rothesay, Sackville, Moncton, and the Acadian shore at Bouctouche, Richibucto, Rexton
# and Caraquet, with Kedgwick in the north-west. Region nb, claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest.
# Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for every place (historicplaces.ca, read
# with tools/page.py --all); Wikipedia's list of National Historic Sites in New Brunswick and its lists of historic places
# by county, read with tools/page.py; Wikipedia raw text for the Rothesay station; and web search results that quote the
# managing bodies' own pages for opening times. Pins placed from the ArcGIS geocoder's address points or the Register's
# coordinate, then checked on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'nb_mdvp','label':'Saint Andrews to Caraquet: churches, courthouses and museums of New Brunswick','added':'2026-11-24 21:30'}
PLACES=[
N('greenock-church-st-andrews','Greenock Church','Saint Andrews','nb','worship','standing',45.07678,-67.05362,'exact','view','walk','','1821–1824',
  'Built in 1821 to 1824 by the local builder Donald D. Morrison with money from the merchant Christopher Scott, Greenock Church marks the growth of the Church of Scotland in New Brunswick. It is a National Historic Site of Canada, and the Register calls it a well-preserved example of the last stage in the change from the meeting house to the church plan.',
  'A wooden Palladian-style church with a classical porch and a staged spire. The Register describes an open two-storey interior with box pews, a high carved pulpit, a gallery on three sides held up by Ionic pillars, and birdseye maple and mahogany fittings; the oak carving on the steeple is said to stand for Greenock in Scotland. Whether the public may go in outside services was not found.',
  'A Palladian meeting-house church with its box pews and high pulpit intact.',
  'An active church at 134 Montague Street in the St. Andrews town grid; look at it from the street. No service times or visiting hours were found on a page of the congregation\'s, so none are given. Parking not checked: street parking in town.',
  [HP('Greenock Church National Historic Site of Canada',12042),HP('Greenock Presbyterian Church',17121)],brick='no'),
N('charlotte-county-courthouse-gaol-st-andrews','Charlotte County Court House and Gaol','Saint Andrews','nb','civic','standing',45.07563,-67.04922,'exact','open','walk','','1832–1840',
  'The Charlotte County Gaol of 1832 is a two-storey stone building that served as the county prison for nearly 150 years. The wooden court house beside it was built in 1839 and 1840 and is, the Register says, the best preserved example in New Brunswick of the typical mid-19th-century Maritime court house. Together they are a National Historic Site of Canada.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey wooden court house with a pedimented portico, and behind it the small stone gaol on the same elevated lot. The gaol now houses the Charlotte County Archives and is shown to visitors in summer. The Register does not describe the interior; not checked.',
  'A little court house and its 1832 stone gaol, still side by side.',
  'The gaol is run by the Charlotte County Archives at 123 Frederick Street; the court house on the same lot is a county building. Walk up from the street. Hours are those the Archives gave for summer (a web search result quoting its page); the page itself was not opened, so call 506-529-4248 before a trip outside June to August. Parking not checked: street parking.',
  [HP('Charlotte County Courthouse and Gaol National Historic Site of Canada',12582),HP('Charlotte County Gaol',1388),L('Charlotte County Archives and Old Gaol','https://ccarchives.ca/')],brick='no',hours='June to August, Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., closed 1 to 2 p.m.; archives research by appointment (Archives page via search; confirm)'),
N('connell-house-woodstock','Connell House','Woodstock','nb','landmark','standing',46.15089,-67.57516,'exact','open','drive','','about 1840',
  'Charles Connell, a lumber merchant and politician, had this house built about 1840. The Register says that temple-like houses of this kind are rare in Canada, though common in the United States, where Connell, who came of Loyalist stock, may have found the idea. The family lived in it until 1975, when the Carleton County Historical Association bought it for a museum and archives.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey wooden mansion with a double-height columned verandah along the front and one side, in the Greek Revival style. Several additions of the late 1800s and early 1900s hide the temple shape of the first block. It is a museum, with the Tappan Adney room (the man credited with saving the birch-bark canoe) according to the society\'s page.',
  'A Greek Revival temple-front mansion that is now the county museum.',
  'Run as a museum and archives by the Carleton County Historical Society at 128 Connell Street. Hours below are from the society\'s page as quoted in a search result, which also says winter visits are by appointment and that another listing gives Tuesday to Saturday; call 506-328-9706 first. Parking not checked: street parking.',
  [HP('Connell House National Historic Site of Canada',11969),HP('Honourable Charles Connell House',4942),L('Carleton County Historical Society','https://www.cchsnb.ca/?p=6032')],brick='no',hours='Weekdays 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; weekends and winter by appointment (society page via search; another listing says Tuesday to Saturday; call ahead)'),
N('belmont-house-lincoln','Belmont House (Wilmot house)','Lincoln','nb','landmark','standing',45.91372,-66.58724,'area','view','drive','','about 1820',
  'Built about 1820 on the south-west bank of the Saint John River, about 16 kilometres from Fredericton, Belmont House is a large neoclassical country house. It is a National Historic Site of Canada for its style and for Robert Duncan Wilmot (1809 to 1891), a Father of Confederation, who lived there on and off from 1839 until his death.',
  'The Register describes a house whose gable end is shaped to look like a temple front, repeated on the back, with symmetrical, classically ordered façades. Its present use, its owner and the state of the interior were not found, and the pin is the Register\'s point, not checked against the building.',
  'A temple-fronted river house of the 1820s, home of a Father of Confederation.',
  'Ownership and whether the public can visit were not found in any source opened: treat it as private and look only from the public road. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Belmont House / R. Wilmot Home National Historic Site of Canada',1192)],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fredericton-military-compound','Fredericton Military Compound (Garrison District)','Fredericton','nb','fort','standing',45.96275,-66.64056,'site','open','walk','forts','1784–1869',
  'The British garrison at Fredericton was based here from 1784 until 1869, when the new Dominion of Canada took over its own defence. At one point the compound held more than fifty buildings; many burned in a fire in 1825, and the surviving ones were built after it. Four remain: the Soldiers\' Barracks, the Guard House, the Officers\' Quarters and the Militia Arms Store. It is a National Historic Site of Canada.',
  'A group of four early-19th-century military buildings beside the Saint John River, with the open green of Officers Square (about 70 by 90 metres) to the east. The city runs free Guard House tours in summer and the porch is used for a small theatre, according to its listing. Interiors other than the Guard House were not checked.',
  'Four garrison buildings of the 1820s and 1830s round a parade ground.',
  'The square and grounds are public. The Guard House tours are run by the City of Fredericton; the hours below are the summer ones from a city listing as quoted in a search result (the year on it was not shown), so confirm with the city. Parking not checked: street parking and lots in the Garrison District.',
  [HP('Fredericton Military Compound National Historic Site of Canada',18989),L('City of Fredericton: Free Guard House Tours','https://www.fredericton.ca/node/8089')],brick='?',hours='Summer, Guard House tours Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closes 1 p.m. Thursdays (city listing via search; confirm dates)'),
N('rothesay-railway-station','Rothesay Railway Station (European and North American Railway)','Rothesay','nb','rail','standing',45.38944,-65.99917,'site','view','drive','stations','1858–1860',
  'One of the first stations built by the new European and North American Railway on its Saint John to Shediac line, the Rothesay station dates from 1858 to 1860. It was first called Kennebecasis Station and was renamed for a visit by the Prince of Wales, one of whose titles is Rothesay. Wikipedia says it closed in 1975. It is a National Historic Site of Canada as one of a few surviving stations from before 1860, the earliest railway architecture left in the Maritime provinces.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey wooden Neo-Gothic station with the stationmaster\'s quarters upstairs, built to a standard design. What the building is used for now, and whether anyone may go in, was not found.',
  'A Gothic-style 1860 railway station, among the oldest in Canada.',
  'Ownership and present use not found in the sources opened; look from the public road. The pin is Wikipedia\'s coordinate and has not been matched to the building. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Rothesay Railway Station (European and North American) National Historic Site of Canada',7546),W('Rothesay station')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('tilley-house-gagetown','Tilley House (Queens County Museum)','Gagetown','nb','landmark','standing',45.78463,-66.14439,'exact','open','drive','','1786–1817',
  'The south end of this Front Street house was built in 1786 for a Loyalist doctor, Frederick Stickles; the north half was added in 1817 by Thomas Tilley, a carpenter and joiner. Sir Samuel Leonard Tilley, a Father of Confederation, was born in the parlour bedroom in 1818. The house has been the Queens County Museum since 1967 and is a National Historic Site of Canada.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey frame cottage of the early 19th century, typical of Queens County. The grounds hold a heritage herb garden and the national historic site monument. Inside is the county museum; the display was not checked.',
  'A Loyalist cottage where a Father of Confederation was born.',
  'Run as a museum by Queens County Heritage at 69 Front Street; admission by donation, says its page. The page gives conflicting season dates, so call 506-488-2966 or check queenscountyheritage.com. Parking not checked: village street.',
  [HP('Tilley House',15950),L('Queens County Heritage: hours, fees, routes','https://queenscountyheritage.com/inner_hours_fees_routes.php')],brick='?',hours='Mid-June to late August, Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Tuesday and other months by appointment (pages differ; confirm)'),
N('hammond-house-sackville','Hammond House','Sackville','nb','landmark','standing',45.89952,-64.37699,'exact','view','walk','','1896',
  'Designed by Burke and Horwood and built in 1896 for the artist John Hammond, who moved in in March 1897, Hammond House passed later to Mount Allison University. It is a National Historic Site of Canada as a particularly good example of Queen Anne Revival domestic architecture.',
  'A large Queen Anne Revival house in a landscaped setting on the Mount Allison campus, with the fanciful forms, lopsided massing and multicoloured surfaces of the style. What the university uses it for now, and the interior, were not checked.',
  'A showy Queen Anne house of 1896 on a university campus.',
  'On the campus of Mount Allison University at 82 York Street (Register address); look from the campus paths and street. Whether it is open to visitors was not found. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Hammond House National Historic Site of Canada',7421)],brick='?'),
N('free-meeting-house-moncton','Free Meeting House','Moncton','nb','worship','standing',46.09393,-64.77468,'exact','open','walk','','1821',
  'Built by members of the community as a simple meeting house and dedicated in 1821, the Free Meeting House was meant for every denomination and, until 1963, held Protestant, Roman Catholic and Jewish congregations in turn. It was altered over the years and restored in 1990 as a Moncton centennial project to look as it did in 1821. It is a National Historic Site of Canada for what it says about religious toleration in the Maritimes.',
  'A wood-frame building with a symmetrical front and classical details, and inside an open hall with box pews, according to the Register. It is used again for special services by different groups and is open to the public by request.',
  'Moncton\'s first meeting house, open to all churches, with its box pews.',
  'At 20 Mountain Road, next to Resurgo Place, which looks after visits. A Historic Places Days listing says visits are by request at the Resurgo Place admissions desk during its business hours (506-856-4383); no fixed hours were found. The grounds hold an old graveyard. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Free Meeting House National Historic Site of Canada',12029),HP('Free Meeting House',1940),L('Historic Places Days: Free Meeting House','https://historicplacesdays.ca/places/free-meeting-house')],brick='no',hours='By request at the Resurgo Place admissions desk during its business hours (Historic Places Days listing; no fixed hours found)'),
N('kent-museum-convent-bouctouche','Convent of the Immaculate Conception (Kent Museum), Bouctouche','Bouctouche','nb','civic','standing',46.47959,-64.69884,'exact','open','drive','','1880',
  'Father François-Xavier-Joseph Michaud founded and built this convent in 1880 to educate girls, for the Sisters of Charity of the Immaculate Conception and later the Religieuses de Notre-Dame-du-Sacré-Cœur. The Gothic Revival Sacré-Cœur chapel was the work of the master craftsman Léon Léger. It is a provincial historic site and now holds the Kent Museum.',
  'A two-storey Second Empire building with a mansard roof, overlooking Bouctouche Bay, with the chapel built to match it. The museum\'s own page says it is housed in the former convent. What the exhibits and chapel interior hold was not checked.',
  'A mansard-roofed Acadian convent with a chapel by a master carver, now a museum.',
  'The Kent Museum (Musée de Kent) at 150 du Couvent Road; hours below come from the museum\'s page as quoted in a search result, so confirm before going. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Convent of the Immaculate Conception',2498),L('Kent Museum: visit','https://www.museedekent.ca/en/visitez/')],brick='?',hours='July to September, Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.; other times by appointment (museum page via search)'),
N('saint-john-county-court-house','Saint John County Court House','Saint John','nb','civic','standing',45.27377,-66.05705,'exact','view','walk','','1826–1829',
  'Built in 1826 to 1829 to hold courtrooms and offices for the Supreme Court and the Court of Quarter Sessions, with a council chamber for the city council, this three-storey stone court house stands on high ground facing King Square, with the gaol behind it. The Register says it is a typical early-19th-century British public building in Canada, and it was made a National Historic Site in 1974. It still holds the sheriff\'s and coroner\'s office and sits for sessions of the courts.',
  'A Neoclassical stone building with carefully ordered classical details. Across the street are the Loyalist Burial Ground and King Square. The interior was not described in the sources opened and was not checked.',
  'A stone Neoclassical court house of 1829 on King Square.',
  'A working court house at 20 Sydney Street: look from the square and the street, and go in only on court business. Parking not checked: city street parking.',
  [HP('Saint John County Court House National Historic Site of Canada',7547)],brick='no'),
N('mechanics-engine-house-saint-john','Number 2 Mechanics\' Volunteer Company Engine House','Saint John','nb','civic','standing',45.27346,-66.05695,'exact','view','walk','','1840',
  'Built in 1840, this small stone fire hall facing King Square is the earliest known fire hall in Canada built for hand-pumped fire engines. The Register says it shows both the Neoclassical style of the day and the part volunteer fire companies played in Victorian city life, and it is a National Historic Site of Canada.',
  'A small two-storey stone building with a pitched roof, squeezed between the larger court house and a row of four-storey shops. Its present use and interior were not found.',
  'Canada\'s earliest known hand-engine fire hall, wedged beside the court house.',
  'At 24 Sydney Street. Look from King Square; whether it is open was not found. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Number 2 Mechanics\' Volunteer Company Engine House National Historic Site of Canada',13472)],brick='no'),
N('bonar-law-house-rexton','Bonar Law House','Rexton','nb','landmark','standing',46.64546,-64.8805,'area','view','drive','','1850–1918',
  'This farm on the north bank of the Richibucto River is the birthplace of Andrew Bonar Law, the only British prime minister born outside the United Kingdom. The Law family owned it from 1850 to 1918, and a stone cairn to his memory was put up in 1925. It is a provincial historic site.',
  'The Register describes a plain 19th-century wooden farmhouse facing the river, with a barn and a wagon shed joined by board fencing to make an enclosed courtyard of an unusual British kind, on 8.9 hectares. Whether the house is open and its interior were not found.',
  'A courtyard farm where a British prime minister was born.',
  'At 31 Bonar Law Avenue (Route 116), near the Route 11 junction. Whether the public can enter was not found; look from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Bonar Law House',6215)],brick='no'),
N('saint-pierre-aux-liens-church-caraquet','Église Saint-Pierre-aux-Liens, Caraquet','Caraquet','nb','worship','standing',47.78991,-64.96444,'exact','view','drive','','1857',
  'Built in 1857 in the Neoclassical style with sandstone sawn by Amice Duval, a carpenter and contractor from the island of Jersey, to plans by the Saint John architect Matthew Stead, this Roman Catholic church is among the oldest stone churches in New Brunswick. It was meant to dominate the institutional centre of Caraquet and shows how much a church meant to Acadian communities. Provincial historic site.',
  'A sandstone church in the middle of the town\'s church-and-school quarter on the Boulevard Saint-Pierre Ouest; the Register says nothing of the interior. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1857 sandstone Acadian church by the architect of Saint John\'s Stone Church era.',
  'An active parish church at 213 Saint-Pierre Boulevard West. No service times or visiting hours were found on a page of the parish\'s. Parking not checked: street parking and lots in the church quarter.',
  [HP('Église Saint-Pierre-aux-Liens',2147)],brick='no'),
N('ballast-heaps-richibucto','Ballast Heaps, Richibucto Harbour','Richibucto','nb','curio','traces',46.6879,-64.8548,'area','view','boat','','1790s–1800s',
  'Two small islands in Richibucto Harbour were made by sailing ships dumping their ballast (stone, sand, gravel, and pieces of iron and brick) before loading timber. The Register says the heaps appear on maps as early as 1827, 1829 and 1839, and that their size reflects how long and how busy the port was. The town has listed them as a local historic place.',
  'Two low islands in the harbour, one north across from York Point and one near the mouth of Weldon\'s Creek. They are best seen from the harbour shore. Whether anything can be seen on them but gravel and scrub was not checked.',
  'Islands built by the rubbish of the timber ships.',
  'In the harbour, so reached or seen only from the shore or by boat. Who owns them was not found, and no landing is suggested. The pin is the Register\'s point in the harbour, not either island exactly. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Ballast Heaps',16346)],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('kedgwick-railway-station','Former Kedgwick Railway Station','Kedgwick','nb','rail','standing',47.64338,-67.34947,'exact','view','drive','stations','1919–1920',
  'A Canadian National Railway station built in 1919 and 1920 from the company\'s standard plans, the Kedgwick station is a provincial historic site as a good example of rural railway architecture in New Brunswick. The Register says it stands for the rapid growth of the north of the province in the early 1900s, when the railway opened markets to farm and forest products and brought hunting and fishing visitors.',
  'The former station building near the old tracks, with its front façade visible from the village. What it is used for now was not found.',
  'A standard-plan 1920 station that opened the north of the province.',
  'At 2A Jeanne-d\'Arc Road; look from the road. Ownership and whether it is open not found. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Former Kedgwick Railway Station',2428)],brick='?'),
]
